<% content_for :title, "Buddhism & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="buddhism-addiction-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="buddhism-addiction-hero">
    <picture class="buddhism-addiction-background">
      <source media="(max-width: 767px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('addiction_buddhism_mobile.png') %>">
      <%= image_tag "addiction_buddhism_desktop.png",
          alt: "",
          class: "buddhism-addiction-background-image",
          fetchpriority: "high" %>
    </picture>
    <div class="buddhism-addiction-hero-content">
      <p class="buddhism-addiction-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES / BUDDHISM
      </p>
      <h1>
        <span>ADDICTION</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>BUDDHIST</span>
        <span>LENS.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="buddhism-addiction-intro">
        SUFFERING. CRAVING.<br>
        ACTION. CONSEQUENCE.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 / ANOTHER WAY TO LOOK -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-pink" id="another-way-to-look">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">01 / ANOTHER WAY TO LOOK</p>
      <h2>BEGIN<br>WHERE<br>YOU ARE.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        The Buddha's teachings begin with the recognition of <em>dukkha</em>, often translated as suffering, dissatisfaction, or unsatisfactoriness. Life contains physical pain, emotional distress, disappointment, loss, and circumstances that refuse to conform to what you want. Even pleasurable experiences are impermanent because they cannot be made to last forever.
      </p>

      <p>
        Addiction can become closely connected to suffering. Sometimes you drink because you're experiencing genuine pain. Sometimes you drink because you want pleasure, excitement, or relief from ordinary life. Sometimes the desire arises before you've consciously identified anything you want to escape. Buddhism doesn't require you to pretend your suffering is imaginary. Physical pain is real. Mental illness is real. Fear, grief, and loneliness can be overwhelming. The First Noble Truth asks you to recognize suffering clearly rather than deny it or attempt to make it disappear at any cost. There is, however, a distinction between the suffering that already exists and the additional suffering created by your response to it.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        “From craving springs grief, from craving springs fear.”
        <cite>— Dhammapada</cite>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 / SUFFERING & CRAVING -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-yellow" id="suffering-&-craving">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">02 / SUFFERING & CRAVING</p>
      <h2>WHAT'S<br>ACTUALLY<br>HAPPENING?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        The Second Noble Truth identifies <em>taṇhā</em>, or craving, as a central cause of suffering. Craving includes pursuing pleasurable sensations, wanting to become something different, and wanting unpleasant experiences to disappear. Alcohol can fulfill different purposes at different times. You may crave intoxication itself. You may want to become more confident, less inhibited, or less concerned about your circumstances. Or you may want to escape pain, sadness, anger, boredom, or fear.
      </p>

      <p>
        Aversion involves rejecting experiences you find unpleasant. You don't necessarily drink because you're chasing happiness. Sometimes you're trying to get away from something you cannot tolerate. Craving and aversion can operate together. You crave the relief alcohol promises because you want to escape what you're experiencing. But satisfying craving doesn't necessarily eliminate it. One drink may produce relief, followed by the desire for another. The original suffering remains, while the consequences of drinking may create additional suffering.
      </p>

      <div class="ba-grid">
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>DUKKHA</h3><p>Recognize the suffering that is already present.</p></div>
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>TAṆHĀ</h3><p>Examine the craving that develops in response.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 / THREE POISONS & HEEDLESSNESS -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-purple" id="three-poisons-&-heedlessness">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">03 / THREE POISONS & HEEDLESSNESS</p>
      <h2>WHAT ARE<br>YOU HOLDING<br>ONTO?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        Buddhism identifies greed, aversion, and delusion as the Three Poisons, fundamental roots of unskillful action. Greed involves grasping after pleasure or wanting more. Aversion involves rejecting experiences you find unpleasant. Delusion involves misunderstanding reality, including your thoughts, perceptions, and the consequences of your actions. These three tendencies can reinforce one another in addiction. You may desire alcohol because it feels good, want to escape something painful, and simultaneously convince yourself that the consequences will be manageable. You may believe you need alcohol immediately or that your judgment remains reliable despite intoxication. The Buddhist task isn't to condemn yourself for experiencing these mental states. It's to recognize how they influence your behavior.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Fifth Precept encourages abstaining from intoxicants that cause heedlessness. Heedlessness involves a loss of awareness, ethical restraint, and careful consideration of consequences. Alcohol can interfere with judgment, increase impulsivity, and undermine your ability to act according to your values. You may speak harshly, disregard boundaries, act recklessly, or make decisions that you would recognize as harmful when sober. The Fifth Precept is therefore not simply about avoiding alcohol because it might lead to punishment. It concerns protecting the clarity and awareness necessary for ethical conduct.
      </p>

      <div class="ba-callout">GREED. AVERSION. DELUSION.<br>WHAT DOES ALCOHOL PROMISE?</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / KARMA & AWARENESS -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-black" id="karma-&-awareness">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">04 / KARMA & AWARENESS</p>
      <h2>PAY<br>ATTENTION.<br>GET CURIOUS.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        Karma is often misunderstood as punishment. In Buddhist teachings, it is fundamentally connected to intentional action. Your intentions influence your behavior. Your actions have consequences. Repeated behaviors can strengthen habits that become increasingly difficult to interrupt. You may intend to relieve suffering and instead create more suffering. You may intend to communicate hurt and instead hurt someone else. You may intend to experience pleasure and encounter consequences that last long after the pleasure disappears. Understanding your intention doesn't erase the consequences. Likewise, suffering is not proof that you deserve punishment. Illness, physical pain, and other people's behavior aren't automatically consequences of your past actions. Karma encourages you to examine what you did, why you did it, and what followed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mindfulness, or <em>sati</em>, involves paying attention to the body, feelings, states of mind, and mental phenomena. You can recognize anger without assuming your anger is justified. You can experience craving without treating it as an instruction. You can notice an urgent thought without concluding that immediate action is necessary. The teaching of <em>anicca</em>, or impermanence, reminds you that conditioned experiences change. Pleasure, emotions, thoughts, and cravings don't remain exactly the same forever. This doesn't mean chronic pain or mental illness can simply be meditated away. It means individual experiences are changing even within ongoing difficulties.
      </p>

      <p>
        The teaching of <em>anattā</em>, or not-self, challenges the idea that you possess a permanent, unchanging identity. Your thoughts, emotions, perceptions, and mental states arise through changing conditions. You aren't permanently defined by any single experience or action. However, not-self doesn't mean your actions belong to someone else or that responsibility disappears. It offers a way to recognize the complexity of identity while continuing to acknowledge what you've done.
      </p>

      <div class="ba-stack">
        <div class="ba-stack-item"><span>01</span><div><h3>INTENTION</h3><p>What did you intend to accomplish?</p></div></div>
        <div class="ba-stack-item"><span>02</span><div><h3>ACTION</h3><p>What did you actually do?</p></div></div>
        <div class="ba-stack-item"><span>03</span><div><h3>CONSEQUENCE</h3><p>What suffering or change followed?</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 / COMPASSION & RESPONSIBILITY -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-pink" id="compassion-&-responsibility">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">05 / COMPASSION & RESPONSIBILITY</p>
      <h2>FACE IT<br>WITH SOME<br>COMPASSION.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        The Noble Eightfold Path includes Right Speech and Right Action. Right Speech encourages truthful, beneficial, timely communication that avoids unnecessary harm. Right Action emphasizes ethical behavior and respect for the safety and well-being of others. Intoxication can interfere with both. Buddhist compassion, or <em>karuṇā</em>, involves recognizing suffering and wishing for its alleviation. This includes your suffering and the suffering of people affected by your actions. Compassion isn't the same as making excuses. You can recognize why you behaved a certain way while acknowledging that your behavior caused harm. You can regret your actions without believing that regret itself constitutes accountability.
      </p>

      <div class="ba-grid">
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>UNDERSTANDING</h3><p>Recognize the conditions that influenced your actions.</p></div>
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>RESPONSIBILITY</h3><p>Acknowledge the harm without making excuses.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 / THE MIDDLE WAY -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-yellow" id="the-middle-way">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">06 / THE MIDDLE WAY</p>
      <h2>TAKE IT<br>INTO YOUR<br>LIFE.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        The Middle Way avoids both indulgence as a solution to suffering and extreme self-punishment. It doesn't mean that everything should be consumed in moderation, nor does it require you to stop experiencing pleasure. It encourages you to examine your relationship with desire, discomfort, and the impulse to change how you feel. You cannot eliminate suffering from life, but you can work toward reducing the suffering you create through your responses to it.
      </p>

      <div class="ba-grid">
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>RIGHT SPEECH</h3><p>What do your words do to others?</p></div>
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>RIGHT ACTION</h3><p>What consequences follow your behavior?</p></div>
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>MINDFULNESS</h3><p>What is happening in your mind right now?</p></div>
        <div class="ba-box"><h3>COMPASSION</h3><p>Can understanding coexist with accountability?</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 / ALCOHOL DIARIES THROUGH A BUDDHIST LENS -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-black ba-stories-intro" id="alcohol-diaries">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">07 / THE ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>THE<br>STORIES.<br>THE LENS.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        Each story begins in different circumstances, but the Buddhist
        teachings illuminate how suffering, craving, heedlessness,
        and consequences can connect them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 08 / FIRST DUI -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-yellow ba-story-section" id="first-dui">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">08 / ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>FIRST<br>DUI.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p class="ba-story-label">A BUDDHIST INTERPRETATION</p>
      <div class="ba-callout">PAIN. RELIEF.<br>MORE SUFFERING.</div>
      <p>
        Severe sciatica pain and frustration led you to seek relief through
        alcohol. Watching an older cyclist brought your lost physical freedom
        into focus, intensifying your resistance to your circumstances.
        Through a Buddhist lens, that resistance reflects aversion, while
        the growing desire for alcohol reflects craving. Drinking temporarily
        eased your pain, but reinforced the urge to drink more and led to
        consequences for you and others. The suffering was real; it explains
        the desire for relief without excusing drinking and driving.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 09 / THIRD DUI -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-purple ba-story-section" id="third-dui">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">09 / ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>THIRD<br>DUI.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p class="ba-story-label">A BUDDHIST INTERPRETATION</p>
      <div class="ba-callout">JACKPOT.<br>NO.<br>GUESS WHO WON?</div>
      <p>
        Mania, restlessness, and impaired judgment intensified your craving
        for alcohol. Discovering that the interlock was not requesting
        additional tests brought the thoughts <em>Jackpot</em> and <em>No</em>
        into conflict, but the urge to drink dominated. Buddhism invites you
        to examine craving, heedlessness, and the difference between noticing
        an impulse and acting wisely. It does not erase the medical reality
        of mania or your responsibility for the consequences. Earlier warning
        signs, support, and a treatment plan matter when awareness alone
        is not enough.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 10 / PRESS SEND -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-pink ba-story-section" id="press-send">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">10 / ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>PRESS<br>SEND.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p class="ba-story-label">A BUDDHIST INTERPRETATION</p>
      <div class="ba-callout">RIGHT SPEECH.<br>WRONG HOUR.</div>
      <p>
        Intoxication intensified emotional distress and distorted how you
        interpreted your relationships. You craved reassurance, resisted
        feelings of rejection, and sometimes sent messages intended to hurt
        the people you wanted to reach. Buddhism's teaching of Right Speech
        asks whether words are truthful, timely, and helpful. The resulting
        conflict created more suffering, while the next morning brought
        shame and regret. Compassion makes room for your pain, but
        accountability means acknowledging the harm, respecting boundaries,
        and accepting that forgiveness is not yours to demand.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 11 / THE BIGGER PICTURE -->
  <section class="ba-section ba-black" id="the-bigger-picture">
    <div class="ba-heading">
      <p class="ba-number">11 / THE BIGGER PICTURE</p>
      <h2>LOOK<br>AT THE<br>PATTERN.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-copy">
      <p>
        These stories reveal different circumstances but related patterns:
        physical pain, manic restlessness, and emotional distress each
        contributed to a desire for alcohol or its effects. Craving and
        aversion narrowed your attention; intoxication weakened judgment;
        actions produced consequences beyond the original suffering.
        Buddhist mindfulness, impermanence, karma, and the Noble Eightfold
        Path offer ways to examine those patterns without denying the
        realities of chronic pain, bipolar disorder, or addiction.
        Compassion does not cancel responsibility. You cannot undo the
        past, but you can recognize its patterns and act differently
        in the present.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="buddhism-addiction-ending">
    <div class="ba-ending-heading">
      <p class="ba-ending-small">UNDERSTANDING TAKES PRACTICE.</p>
      <h2>KEEP<br>LOOKING<br><span>CLOSER.</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ba-ending-copy">
      <p>You cannot change what happened by wishing it had been different. You can examine what happened without denial, acknowledge the consequences, and decide what actions to take now.</p>
      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION PERSPECTIVES",
          addiction_perspectives_path,
          class: "ba-back-button" %>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

